{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-reasoning", "file": "references/separator-object-reconciliation.md", "content": "# Separator-and-object demos: reconcile before applying

When demos contain a thin separator color (such as a full row/column of 2), a uniform background, and a small colored object, do not infer that the separator itself should change or that the object simply fills its row/column. Record cell-level differences between input and output: whether the separator is preserved, which background line changes color, and whether the object moves relative to the separator. A demo can encode a coupled positional operation—for example, moving the object across the separator while recoloring an adjacent background line. Apply the complete relationship, not just one salient changed line.

For each test, map coordinates and orientation from the demonstrated relationship rather than copying the demo's absolute row/column. If evidence permits multiple positional mappings with different test predictions, spend a demo request only if it can distinguish them. After incorrect feedback, explicitly discard the failed mapping and revisit all changed and preserved cells; do not repeat it or make an unsupported minimal variation.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-reasoning/references/separator-object-reconciliation.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-evidence-checkpoint", "file": "references/heterogeneous-demo-reconciliation.md", "content": "# Reconcile heterogeneous or seemingly contradictory demos

With costly demos that vary in size, palette, and visual structure, compare each input/output pair at the exact changed-cell level before choosing an action. Record preserved cells/regions and unchanged pairs as constraints. Normalize semantic color roles only where the edits support the mapping; superficial resemblance to a known task or a remembered reference is not validation.

Test a proposed shared rule against every example, separating trigger, selected extent, and replacement mapping. If some pairs appear incompatible, state that uncertainty instead of ignoring inconvenient examples. After a rejected submission, reject the whole compound hypothesis and rebuild the evidence ledger; do not switch transformation families based solely on unrelated skill notes or make unsupported local edits. Spend another demo only when explicit surviving alternatives predict materially different test outputs and the information justifies its cost. Maintain the required single-action JSON protocol.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-demo-evidence-checkpoint/references/heterogeneous-demo-reconciliation.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-evidence-checkpoint", "file": "references/panel-exception-lesson.md", "content": "# Panel classification: exceptions and post-rejection audit

In separator-panel tasks, correct panel dimensions do not validate a marker-presence label rule. Across every demonstration, make a full table of panels and outputs, recording marker presence, count, and arrangement. A marked panel may share the background output label while another marked panel receives the special label; train the signature against all examples rather than extrapolating from early uniformly labeled demos.

For a rejected output, treat the whole mapping as suspect. Recheck panel extraction and reading order, then revisit what feature distinguishes labels. Do not merely edit a few cells based on binary feedback. Request another example only if surviving signatures predict different useful outcomes and the cost is justified; otherwise apply the strongest candidate consistently to all test panels.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-demo-evidence-checkpoint/references/panel-exception-lesson.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-costly-demo-analysis", "file": "references/panel-extraction-and-dimension-check.md", "content": "# Panel extraction and output-dimension validation

When demonstrations have unusually wide/tall inputs and much smaller outputs, inspect the entire layout before assuming a cellwise transformation. Look for a separator line/column and distinct panels on either side. Across demos, compare output dimensions to the panel dimensions: if the output consistently matches one panel, the task likely extracts or transforms that panel while discarding the other, separator, or framing. Normalize colors by role and test candidate panel selection against every example.

After inferring the selected panel, determine its transformation independently from the discarded region. Do not let random-looking cells elsewhere in the input distract from a compact output panel. Apply the same spatial crop/projection to the test input, then map colors using the demos. Validate the resulting row count and each row width against the inferred output dimensions before emitting JSON. Incorrect feedback may stem from a wrong selection rule, a wrong transformation, or simply malformed output dimensions; distinguish these failure classes before spending another attempt.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-costly-demo-analysis/references/panel-extraction-and-dimension-check.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-costly-demo-analysis", "file": "references/instance-learning-panel-rule.md", "content": "# Learning panel-label rules from compressed outputs

When a separator-delimited grid shrinks to one output cell per panel, the lattice and ordering are only the first inference step. A plausible grid with matching dimensions can still be wrong because the cell-label semantics are underdetermined.

## Panel evidence ledger

For every demonstration, align each panel with its output coordinate and record:
- marker/background/separator colors;
- all exceptional-cell coordinates, counts, and arrangement;
- whether features align across panel rows or columns;
- the output value for that panel.

Normalize palettes across examples. Test panel-local rules (presence, count, shape, location) and cross-panel rules (aligned feature sequences, repeated rows/columns, or spatial configurations) against every cell of every output. Distinguish selecting a panel from assigning its output label; the output can encode a relationship rather than copy the local marker color.

## Costly action checkpoint

After each demo, update the ledger before requesting another. Name the competing rules and whether they produce different predictions on the active test. After an incorrect submission, preserve only validated structural facts (such as panel dimensions/order), discard the rejected semantic mapping, and rebuild from all demonstrations. Binary feedback identifies no particular cell, so do not make arbitrary local edits. Spend another demo only when it can discriminate live rules enough to justify its fixed cost.

Always preserve the strict one-action JSON protocol when the evaluator requires it.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-costly-demo-analysis/references/instance-learning-panel-rule.md"}

